<Page
  title="Products running on CosmOS — Sonical"
  description="CosmOS is Sonical's operating system for the ears. It runs on headphones, headsets, earbuds and smart eyewear, and turns them into devices that can take on new abilities after they ship."
  canonical="https://www.sonical.ai/products/"
  eyebrow="Products"
  heading='Products running <span class="acc">on CosmOS</span>'
  nav=""
>
  <p class="lede">
    Sonical is a software company. We build CosmOS, the operating system for the
    ears, and license it to the companies that make audio devices. This page
    covers what CosmOS runs on, what it adds, and which products ship with it today.
  </p>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What a CosmOS product is</h2>
    <p>
      Most audio devices ship frozen in time. The hardware could do more, but the
      software is locked, so the device does the same thing on its last day as on
      its first.
    </p>
    <p>
      CosmOS runs on the device itself, across audio, sensors and every data stream
      the hardware carries. It handles real-time scheduling, lets several apps
      process the same stream, and holds latency low enough for professional
      monitoring. A device running CosmOS can take on new abilities years after it
      was bought. We call these <b>personal hearing computers</b>, and the shift
      they represent <b>Headphone&nbsp;3.0</b>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What it runs on</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="rowcard">
        <h3>Headphones</h3>
        <p>Studio, gaming and everyday listening.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rowcard">
        <h3>Headsets</h3>
        <p>Calls and communication.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rowcard">
        <h3>Earbuds</h3>
        <p>True wireless, all day.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rowcard">
        <h3>Smart eyewear</h3>
        <p>Audio built into glasses.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>What it adds</h2>
    <p>
      Developers publish audio apps to the cloud. They download through the phone
      the listener already owns, onto the CosmOS device in their ears. Apps
      running on CosmOS today:
    </p>
    <ul class="ticks">
      <li>Hearing protection and hearing correction</li>
      <li>Personal audio and noise reduction</li>
      <li>Spatial audio and EQ</li>
      <li>Compressor and crossfeed</li>
      <li>Clarity boost</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      More are on the way. The owner chooses which apps their device runs, so it
      fixes what matters to them rather than only what every product ships with.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Shipping with CosmOS today</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Remora</b> is the personal hearing computer. It is built by Remora, a
      separate product company, and it runs CosmOS. Remora sells two devices:
      <b>Remora&nbsp;ONE</b>, which is wired, and <b>Remora&nbsp;PRO</b>, which is
      wireless over Ultra-Wideband. Specifications, availability and ordering are
      all on Remora's own site.
    </p>
    <div class="ctarow">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://remora.pro" rel="noopener">See Remora at remora.pro →</a>
    </div>
    <!-- TODO: add a second named product maker here once one is public. Until
         then this section reads as a single-product list, which under-sells the
         platform. Revisit per the open question in the project context doc
         ("Products on CosmOS" section, §9 #8). -->
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>Building a product on CosmOS</h2>
    <p>
      Device makers, technology suppliers and app developers all come in through
      the same door: the CosmOS Certified Technology Program.
    </p>
    <div class="ctarow">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/partners/">The partner program →</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/?reason=products#contact">Talk to us →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
